    "q-344594": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344594",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the **exact** values of $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x-6$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $6$ are\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and  $\\,c=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(3{x}^{2}-2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & ={\\frac{1}{c}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-6x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}[({c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-6c)-0] \\\\ 6c & ={c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-6c \\\\ 0 & ={c}^{3}-{c}^{2}-12c \\\\ 0 & ={c}^{2}-c-12 \\\\ 0 & =(c-4)(c+3) \\\\ c & =-3,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in **ascending order**, the values of $c$ are \n\n$$\nc=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,c=4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
