    "q-18144": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18144",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14153257/assets/d661fd02727800f40ccaaf7f388b99b61b9e3a2d6c2da351c47b2166d1182b48.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[-3,6]$, is shown above. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $40$, $20$ and $10,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,6].$",
      "worked_solution": "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=40-20+10=30.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-(-3)}\\cdot (30) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
