    "q-18135": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18135",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14153257/assets/cc31e5e85263f5a08a10c73c76cf5ba5eec2c27195159da31ef96a614efba7ed.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-2,4],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4].$",
      "worked_solution": "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
